      a: "Because 'own institution' is a per-row reference: the average isn't one number, it's 6 numbers (one per institution), selected by which institution the outer row belongs to. The shape: HAVING COUNT(*) > (SELECT AVG(c.calls) FROM (per-endpoint fold) c WHERE c.supplier_id = p.supplier_id) — the inner query re-runs per outer group, reading the outer's supplier_id each time. What the correlation buys: a benchmark that moves with the row — an endpoint is above average RELATIVE TO ITS OWN BANK's traffic, not the global mean (where Midwest's 24-call endpoints would crush a threshold set by Andes' 12s). Cost honesty: correlated = re-execution per outer row, fine at 16 endpoints, a planner event at millions — the rewrite is a JOIN onto a pre-aggregated per-institution fold, same results, one pass. At Plaid 'above their own institution's average' is exactly how load imbalance inside one bank is found — the global average hides it.",
      tag: "subqueries",
